A single executing Queueable can add only **one** job with `System.enqueueJob`. The 50-job limit applies only to synchronous transactions. In an asynchronous context such as a Queueable, Batch or future method, each parent can have only one child.

The loop over ten regions would therefore fail on the second `System.enqueueJob` call with `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 2`. Edition doesn't change this. In Enterprise Edition and production orgs there is no limit on chain depth, but each parent still gets one child. Developer Edition and Trial orgs also cap the chain depth at 5.

To fan out ten regions, either:
- enqueue all ten jobs from a synchronous context, such as a trigger, controller or anonymous Apex, where up to 50 are allowed, or
- chain them, so each job processes one region and enqueues the next:

```apex
public class RegionSyncJob implements Queueable {
    private List<String> regions;
    public RegionSyncJob(List<String> regions) { this.regions = regions; }

    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        String current = regions.remove(0);
        // ... sync 'current' region ...
        if (!regions.isEmpty()) {
            System.enqueueJob(new RegionSyncJob(regions)); // only one child allowed
        }
    }
}
```

Answer: 1 (only one child job can be enqueued from an executing Queueable)
Source: https://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.apexcode.meta/apexcode/apex_queueing_jobs.htm

